{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787685208,"nonce":"Km47FxRKwUS5jC/ZNWahMtgBETM=","title":"Committed packs are still never deleted (the reclaimer's STOP LINE). ec294b7 shipped the gate — fence-grain borrow edges (`depot_fence_borrows`, written atomically in every success record's transaction, keyed on the record's full published closure) plus the `depot_borrow_tracking_epoch` backfill floor and the refined residue sweep (success records posted \u003e= epoch live until fence expiry; that is what makes \"borrower still has a record\" a usable predicate). This ticket builds the retention pass itself, wired to RetentionProfile TTLs (ADR-0065).","message":"THE DELETION CONTRACT (defined by ec294b7; build exactly this):\nFence F is deletable when its run is terminal and past its retention class's TTL, AND NOT EXISTS a borrow edge on F whose borrower still has a drain record (`depot_fence_borrows b JOIN depot_drain_records r ON r.fence_key = b.borrower_fence WHERE b.owner_fence = F`) — borrower-record lifetime = borrower-fence lifetime; the edge's `run` column keeps the retention join alive as insurance. Deletion is one transaction, POINTERS ONLY, in this order: FOR UPDATE F's `depot_packs` rows FIRST (the drain path's in-txn re-check takes FOR SHARE OF p on the same rows — this lock order is what serializes the two passes in both directions; both orders are already pinned by tests in workspaced.rs), then re-check borrowers, then DELETE F's `depot_pack_members`, `depot_packs`, `depot_drain_records` row, and `depot_fence_borrows WHERE borrower_fence = F` (F's outbound pins die with F, transitively freeing its owners on a later pass).\n\nONE VICTIM FENCE PER TRANSACTION (audit A7): never batch fences into one txn. On deadlock (40P01) the expiry side SKIPS the pass (next cadence retries); the record side surfaces its 40P01 as a 500 and the drain's re-POST is idempotent. The reclaimer's advisory lock (`PACK_RECLAIM_ADVISORY_LOCK`) serializes this pass across replicas the same way.\n\nTHE EPOCH FLOOR'S CLOCK: `depot_borrow_tracking_epoch.epoch` was stamped ONCE, from Postgres `now()`, at the moment migration 0048 ran — one clock authority, never a replica's. Committed expiry must additionally refuse to delete ANY committed pack while a live success record with `posted_at \u003c epoch` exists (indexed EXISTS on `depot_drain_records_posted_at`): pre-epoch records may silently borrow from anything. `posted_at` itself is replica-stamped, so skew can only misclassify a record as pre-epoch — which HOLDS expiry, the safe direction. Time heals: pre-epoch success records keep the 24h-scale TTL sweep, and once the last one is gone the floor costs nothing.\n\nBYTES: this pass never touches the bucket. Deleting F's rows makes its packs rowless and quiet; the bytes flow through the SHIPPED rowless reclaimer (`reclaim_orphan_packs_once`, git-bug ad79c90) behind its two-observation grace, at least one cadence after the pointers.\n\nRETENTION CLASSES: THE PIN WINS — a short-TTL pack pinned by a long-TTL borrower stays; the cost is attribution, not waste; the remedy, if ever measured to matter, is copy-forward compaction LATER, not first.\n\nThe STOP LINE in workspaced.rs moves from \"committed rows are never deleted\" to \"committed rows are deleted only via the borrow check above\" when this lands. The 0044 migration header carries the same warning at the schema.","files":null}]}